/* Modern theme for the login dialog -- fully programmatic, flat colors/vector shapes instead of
   legacy art. Same class names/structure as legacy throughout. Originally styled against a real
   MU Online reference screenshot's dark warm-bronze/crimson RPG dialog look; the frame/panel
   material itself was later cooled to gray/metallic (steel highlights instead of warm-bronze
   ones) -- text (title/body), the checkbox, the header glow, and the primary button's crimson
   fill deliberately stayed warm/gold/crimson, only the structural frame material went cool. No
   ::before/::after or box-shadow to substitute for here -- every effect below is a primitive this
   engine's render interface actually implements: per-side border-*-color bevels for depth, real
   extra <div>s (.frame-accent/.login-content/.header-glow/.separator, added to login.rml) for
   decorative elements, font-effect:outline for text-shadow (unregistered on this engine),
   decorator:linear-gradient/radial-gradient (both need RenderInterface_SDL_GPU's
   CompileShader/RenderShader) for fills and glows. Outer drop-shadow (blur outside the box) and
   box-shadow/filter/backdrop-filter have no substitute at all and are simply not reproduced --
   this engine leaves layer/filter compositing unimplemented.

   Color roles: the title header has a yellow-to-red radial glow behind the title (.header-glow,
   no separate flanking-line elements -- tried and removed, the glow alone read better). The
   primary button (.btn, OK) shares that same crimson family for its own base fill (a vertical
   linear-gradient, bright top to dark maroon bottom, no glow layer of its own -- tried, didn't
   read well, removed) -- so the glow/button/text stay one warm/crimson family, sitting on top of
   the now-cool gray/metallic frame material rather than being part of it. */

body {
	width: 100%;
	height: 100%;
	margin: 0;
	pointer-events: none;
}

/* Outer frame -- position/size/font/border-width/radius stay local (window-specific structural
   values, never carried by the shared class per its own doc comment); the gradient fill and
   per-side border-color bevel come from base.rcss's .modern-frame-crimson (applied in login.rml
   alongside .modern-frame itself) instead of being redeclared here. See the NOTE below for why
   .checkbox-box/.btn stay separately self-contained rather than also being deduped against
   base.rcss. */
#panel {
	position: absolute;
	width: 329dp;
	height: 245dp;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
	font-size: 12dp;
	color: token(text-body);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-radius: 7dp;
}

/* Thin accent line just inside the frame -- structure/position local, palette from base.rcss's
   .modern-frame-accent-crimson (applied in login.rml). Spans the whole panel, behind the header
   text/lines AND .login-content below. */
.frame-accent {
	left: 4dp;
	right: 4dp;
	top: 4dp;
	bottom: 4dp;
	border-radius: 5dp;
}

/* Recessed content surface -- starts below the (unboxed) header, not full-inset like
   .modern-panel's own documented default (top:4dp on all sides) -- overridden locally, exactly
   the "leaves room for a header above" case base.rcss's own comment on .modern-panel anticipates.
   Palette from .modern-panel-crimson (applied in login.rml). */
.login-content {
	left: 10dp;
	right: 10dp;
	top: 44dp;
	bottom: 10dp;
	border-radius: 4dp;
}

.modern-inset {
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-metal); /* cool steel-gray frame border, was warm bronze */
	border-top-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 170); /* recessed: dark top/left */
	border-left-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 170);
}
.modern-inset.focused {
	border-color: token(accent-steel);
}

.label {
	position: absolute;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
}
.label-server {
	left: 111dp;
	top: 14dp;
	color: token(text-title);
	font-weight: bold;
	letter-spacing: 1.5px;
	font-effect: outline(1px #000000);
}
.label-account { left: 30dp; top: 73dp; }
.label-password{ left: 30dp; top: 106dp; }

/* Position/size only -- the gradient itself is base.rcss's shared .title-glow now (login.rml
   applies both classes: class="title-glow header-glow"), the first extraction of this pattern for
   reuse across every other dialog/window that has a title. Sits behind .label-server (declared
   first, base.rcss/login.rcss paint-order convention: earlier siblings paint first). See
   base.rcss's own .title-glow comment for the gradient's tuning rationale. */
.header-glow {
	left: 40dp;
	right: 40dp;
	top: 8dp;
	height: 26dp;
}

/* Border-only outline standing in for the legacy input-box frame -- no background, so it never
   covers the legacy input text drawn underneath (border-COLOR comes from .modern-inset, applied
   alongside this class in login.rml -- deliberately no background here for the same reason).
   LoginWin.cpp::SetPosition() positions the real CUITextInputBox text a fixed few px below/right
   of this frame's own top/left (72/105 vs this rule's 66/99 -- text padding within the frame, a
   pre-existing relationship, not a typo) and now scales that offset by the same ratio this file's
   dp values respond to -- keep the two in sync if either changes (see that function's own
   comment). */
.input-frame {
	position: absolute;
	width: 156dp;
	height: 23dp;
	border-radius: token(radius-md)dp;
	pointer-events: none;
}
.input-account  { left: 109dp; top: 66dp; }
.input-password { left: 109dp; top: 99dp; }

.checkbox-row {
	position: absolute;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
.checkbox-remember-me  { left: 109dp; top: 134dp; }
.checkbox-save-password{ left: 109dp; top: 158dp; }

/* NOTE: login.rml now links base.rcss too (see the frame/panel/accent rules above),
   but .checkbox-box/.btn below are deliberately left as their own full, self-contained copies
   rather than deduped against base.rcss's now-identical .checkbox-box/.checkbox-box.checked and
   .btn+.btn-ok -- this pass stayed scoped to the frame primitives specifically. They're provably
   the same values today (base.rcss's copies were retrofitted from these exact ones), so deduping
   is safe whenever it's actually worth doing, just not bundled into this pass. */
.checkbox-box {
	display: inline-block;
	position: relative;
	width: 16dp;
	height: 16dp;
	background-color: #0a0d10;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #1a2027, #0a0d10);
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: token(border-metal);
	border-top-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 180);
	border-radius: 3dp;
}
.checkbox-box.checked {
	background-color: #6b5220;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, token(accent-gold), #6b5220); /* bright stop = accent-gold exactly; dark stop is a derived shade, no token */
	border-color: token(accent-gold);
}

/* No CSS transform rotation support in this build -- checkmark built from 5 small axis-aligned
   "pixels" stair-stepping into a blocky tick shape. */
.tick-px {
	position: absolute;
	width: 2dp;
	height: 2dp;
	background-color: #141a1f;
}
.tick-px-1 { left: 3dp;  top: 7dp; }
.tick-px-2 { left: 5dp;  top: 9dp; }
.tick-px-3 { left: 7dp;  top: 7dp; }
.tick-px-4 { left: 9dp;  top: 5dp; }
.tick-px-5 { left: 11dp; top: 3dp; }

.checkbox-tick {
	display: none;
	position: absolute;
	left: 0; top: 0; width: 16dp; height: 16dp;
}
.checkbox-box.checked .checkbox-tick {
	display: block;
}

.checkbox-label {
	position: absolute;
	left: 21dp;
	top: 1dp;
	white-space: nowrap;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
}

/* Gradient-to-transparent divider above the button row (mockup's .separator). */
.separator {
	position: absolute;
	left: 20dp;
	right: 20dp;
	top: 186dp;
	height: 1dp;
	pointer-events: none;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, token(border-metal) 15%, token(accent-steel) 50%, token(border-metal) 85%, transparent); /* was warm bronze; structural framing goes cool now, matching the frame material */
}

/* Crimson "primary action" button (OK) -- reuses the exact same 4-stop vertical gradient as the
   title banner's own linear-gradient (bright top, dark maroon bottom), for the same color instead
   of a separate bronze/gold fill. .btn-cancel below overrides this to the mockup's own neutral
   secondary treatment. No glow layer (tried a radial-gradient sheen here, both a circle and a thin
   ellipse -- didn't read well, removed; the flat gradient fill plus the bright border-top-color
   bevel below is the whole effect). */
.btn {
	position: absolute;
	width: 54dp;
	height: 30dp;
	text-align: center;
	vertical-align: middle;
	line-height: 30dp;
	cursor: pointer;
	color: #f0e0bc;
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
	font-effect: outline(1px #000000);
	background-color: #430e17;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, token(accent-crimson-bright), token(accent-crimson) 35%, #6c1520 70%, #430e17); /* bright/base stops are real tokens; the two darker stops are derived shades with no token of their own */
	border-width: 1dp;
	border-color: #0a0706;
	border-top-color: rgba(211, 74, 94, 115); /* accent-crimson-bright's rgb @ 115 alpha */
	border-radius: 5dp;
	pointer-events: auto;
}
.btn:hover {
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #e05f72, token(accent-crimson-bright) 35%, #8c1e2c 70%, #571219); /* #e05f72 is a brighter-than-token hover-only highlight, no token */
	border-color: #e05f72;
}
.btn:active {
	/* Shifts down 2dp to read as physically pressed -- both .btn-ok/.btn-cancel share the same
	   top:196dp below, so one absolute value covers both. Native :active pseudo-class, unverified
	   against this branch's own mouse-input routing (RmlUi's default SDL translation is bypassed
	   here for unrelated reasons). Degrades gracefully if it never
	   fires -- worst case the button just doesn't visibly depress. */
	top: 198dp;
	border-top-color: rgba(165, 35, 54, 65); /* accent-crimson's rgb @ 65 alpha */
	border-bottom-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 200);
}
.btn-ok { left: 150dp; top: 196dp; }

/* Neutral secondary button (Cancel) -- overrides .btn's crimson fill/border, single flat-gray
   decorator instead, keeps its bevel/hover/active mechanics. Must stay textually after
   .btn/.btn:hover/.btn:active above (same specificity, source order decides). */
.btn-cancel {
	left: 211dp;
	top: 196dp;
	background-color: #171a1e;
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #30363c, #20252a 40%, #171a1e 75%, #0e1013); /* cool neutral, was warm charcoal -- structural framing goes cool now */
	border-color: token(border-metal);
}
.btn-cancel:hover {
	decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #454d54, #30363c 40%, #20252a 75%, #171a1e);
	border-color: token(accent-steel);
}

.trust-warning {
	position: absolute;
	left: 30dp;
	top: 252dp;
	width: 280dp;
	color: token(semantic-warning);
	font-family: "token(font-body)";
}
